{"kind":"report","text":"REVIVE-WITH-CHANGES on 6c365642: the fast test lane at 66491de1 is worth reviving for its fixture sharing, which is a large measured win at zero coverage cost, but it cannot land as written. Two blockers and one coverage cut I would not ship.\n\nTHE PAYOFF IS REAL AND I MEASURED IT, NOT ESTIMATED IT. This machine was contended throughout by other agents, and wall clock varied up to 1.9x for identical work, so the load-insensitive metric is git subprocess counts, which were bit-identical across every repeat. internal/kernel: base 7609 git procs and 124s, branch 3741 procs and 10s, same 110 tests and 0 skips. cmd/gs TestReviewGuard: 1918 procs and 28-53s, branch 1084 procs and about 5.4s, same 10 tests. Current main plus the branch on kernel: 9674 procs and 138s down to 7026 procs and 30s, all passing -- 4.4x. The payoff has GROWN since the branch was cut, because cmd/gs went from 21 plus 10 fixtures to 33 plus 14.\n\nBLOCKER ONE: IT PANICS internal/app ON CURRENT MAIN. The branch injects an implicit t.Parallel() inside testRepo, newFixtureWithCeiling and newWorkflowFixture. Main has since added two tests that call t.Setenv and then testRepo -- app_test.go:1229 and :1620 -- and t.Setenv with t.Parallel is a hard panic: 'testing: test using t.Setenv, t.Chdir, or cryptotest.SetGlobalRandom can not use t.Parallel'. app_test.go auto-merges cleanly and then panics the package. I verified the injected helper and both call sites directly. This is a trap, not a one-time conflict: any future test adding t.Setenv panics the whole package, and the branch's own escape hatch is a strings.Contains on a test name. Main walked into it twice in eleven days without knowing the branch existed. Explicit t.Parallel() at call sites instead.\n\nBLOCKER TWO: THE 30s DEADLINE IS RED TWICE OUT OF TWO. make test at the branch head exits 1 both runs, with 'panic: test timed out after 30s' in cmd/gitseq-mcp, cmd/gs and internal/docset every time. The deadline was calibrated on an idle reference runner; here it converts machine load into a false red. Raise it above the honest worst case or scope it per package.\n\nTHE COVERAGE CUT I WOULD NOT SHIP. TestCheckpointIdentityAndCachedEventGuardsFallBackToFullAudit goes 28.00s to 6.53s, but it gets there by replacing 14 of 17 tamper subtests with a bare validateCheckpoint call. Production does not call that function: internal/kernel/checkpoint.go:373 calls validateCheckpointEventsInto, and validateCheckpoint at :426 is only a wrapper. I checked both. The downgraded cases stop exercising checkpoint publication, the reader's fallback decision, and sequence recovery, so a defect where the validator rejects but the reader trusts the checkpoint anyway, or falls back and returns a short sequence, passes all 14. Subtest counts are identical on both sides, 18 and 18, so no count or skip metric detects the loss. If 28s must come down, shrink the fixture; do not swap the assertion for a wrapper production never uses.\n\nTWO SMALLER ITEMS. The delta tiers in TestReaderLoadsColdThenVerifiesDescendantDeltas go from 1,10,100 to 1,2,3, worth 36s, and that is the only correctness check on the 100-delta tail anywhere -- make perf is a weekly timing comparison, not this assertion. Justify or revert it. And the envelope-fixture hoist measured 5.63s to 5.87s, no saving at all, while coupling the subtests through one shared store; drop it.\n\nTHE REAL PROBLEM IS SOMEWHERE ELSE, AND IT IS BIGGER THAN THIS BRANCH. internal/docset cannot meet any short deadline today. Under -short with all five skips active I stopped it at 5m51s with 23,478 git subprocesses still inside its FIRST test, TestGateEveryNamedActResolvesToALiveRecord, which is not short-skipped and does a full cold verified fold of the roughly 8,600-record log. That cost grows every day the log grows. The branch does not touch it. It deserves its own request and I would rather have that one than this one.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT MEASURE, PLAINLY. The base full-tree go test ./... never finished; I stopped it past ten minutes, still inside docset, at 48,987 git subprocesses. The docset -short run never finished either. I did not measure -race at all, and make race is untouched by the branch, so the -race waits that motivated this request are helped only by the code changes, not by -short.\n\nONE DOCUMENTATION CONSEQUENCE IF THE SKIPS STAY. Six tests become -short skips, and docs/anchoring.md:49 and docs/how-to/end-to-end.md:16 both assert those gates are run by make test. Those pages would need updating with artifact succession, and make docs would need to be in CI so they do not rest only on make race."}